   $q$As you move from left to right, what does the graph of $y = -2x + 6$ do?$q$, $q$[{"text":"It stays level, because it is a horizontal line","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"confused-linear-with-constant"},{"text":"It rises, because the $y$-intercept $6$ is positive","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"judged-direction-by-intercept"},{"text":"It falls, because the slope is $-2$","is_correct":true},{"text":"It rises, because the slope is $2$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"dropped-negative-sign-of-slope"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$It falls, because the slope is $-2$$q$, $q$The slope is the coefficient of $x$, which is $-2$. A negative slope means the line falls as $x$ increases. The sign of the $y$-intercept tells where the line crosses the $y$-axis, not which way it goes.$q$, 'draft', null)
