   $q$Two identical stacks of 30 coins are on a desk. One stack is perfectly straight. The other is pushed so it leans, with every coin still lying flat on the one below it. Which statement about their volumes is true?$q$, $q$[{"text":"The two stacks have equal volumes.","is_correct":true},{"text":"The leaning stack has the greater volume.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"thought-slant-adds-volume"},{"text":"The straight stack has the greater volume.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"thought-slant-removes-volume"},{"text":"The volumes cannot be compared without knowing the lean angle.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"thought-slant-angle-matters"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$The two stacks have equal volumes.$q$, $q$Both stacks have the same height, and at every height the cross section is one coin of the same area. By Cavalieri's principle, solids with equal cross-sectional areas at every height have equal volumes, so the stacks have equal volumes. Leaning the stack moves the coins but does not change how much space each one fills.$q$, 'draft', null)
