   $q$A circle of radius $r$ is cut into many thin, equal sectors. The sectors are placed point up and point down, alternating, to form a shape that is close to a parallelogram. What are the approximate base and height of that parallelogram?$q$, $q$[{"text":"base $\\pi r$, height $2r$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"used-diameter-as-height"},{"text":"base $\\pi r$, height $r$","is_correct":true},{"text":"base $2\\pi r$, height $2r$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"used-circumference-and-diameter"},{"text":"base $2\\pi r$, height $r$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"used-full-circumference-as-base"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$base $\pi r$, height $r$$q$, $q$Half the arcs point down and half point up, so each long side of the shape uses half the circumference, $\frac{2\pi r}{2} = \pi r$. The height of each sector is the radius $r$. So the base is $\pi r$ and the height is $r$, giving area $\pi r \cdot r = \pi r^2$. A base of $2\pi r$ would use the whole circumference along one side.$q$, 'draft', null)
