  ('c7326603-2313-5076-a260-81ec51d1dea0', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'hs-geometry'), null, 'medium',
   $q$For every circle, the circumference divided by the diameter is the same number, $\pi$. Which fact explains why this ratio never changes?$q$, $q$[{"text":"Larger circles curve more sharply than smaller circles.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"confused-curvature-with-scale"},{"text":"All circles have the same area.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"thought-all-circles-congruent"},{"text":"The circumference of a circle is always equal to its diameter.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"thought-circumference-equals-diameter"},{"text":"All circles are similar, so scaling a circle multiplies its circumference and its diameter by the same factor.","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$All circles are similar, so scaling a circle multiplies its circumference and its diameter by the same factor.$q$, $q$Any circle can be dilated onto any other circle, so all circles are similar. A dilation by factor $k$ multiplies every length, including both the circumference and the diameter, by $k$, so their ratio stays the same. Circles of different sizes have different areas, so they are not all the same.$q$, 'draft', null)
