   $q$Solid A is a cylinder with radius $2$ and height $12$. Solid B also has height $12$, and at every height its horizontal cross section has the same area as the cross section of Solid A at that height. What is the volume of Solid B?$q$, $q$[{"text":"$192\\pi$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"used-diameter-as-radius"},{"text":"$48\\pi$","is_correct":true},{"text":"$16\\pi$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"used-cone-formula"},{"text":"$24\\pi$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"forgot-to-square-radius"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$$48\pi$$q$, $q$By Cavalieri's principle, two solids with the same height and equal cross-sectional areas at every level have equal volumes. Solid A has volume $\pi \cdot 2^2 \cdot 12 = 48\pi$, so Solid B also has volume $48\pi$. The value $16\pi$ uses the one third factor, but nothing says Solid B is a cone.$q$, 'draft', null)
