   $q$A cone with radius $3$ cm and height $10$ cm is full of water. All the water is poured into an empty cylinder that also has radius $3$ cm. How deep is the water in the cylinder, in centimeters?$q$, $q$[{"text":"$\\frac{10}{3}$","is_correct":true},{"text":"$10$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"assumed-equal-volumes"},{"text":"$30$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"multiplied-by-three"},{"text":"$5$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"used-one-half"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$$\frac{10}{3}$$q$, $q$The cone holds $\frac{1}{3} \cdot \pi \cdot 9 \cdot 10 = 30\pi$. In the cylinder, $9\pi h = 30\pi$, so $h = \frac{30}{9} = \frac{10}{3}$ cm. This makes sense because a cone holds one third of the cylinder with the same base and height. The value $10$ treats the cone as if it held as much as the cylinder.$q$, 'draft', null)
