  ('33497e74-39b2-5279-9ae8-8d6ed5733f8c', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'hs-geometry'), null, 'hard',
   $q$The area of a regular polygon is $\frac{1}{2} \cdot \text{perimeter} \cdot \text{apothem}$. Regular polygons with more and more sides are inscribed in a circle of radius $r$. As the number of sides grows, the perimeter approaches $2\pi r$ and the apothem approaches $r$. What does the area of the polygons approach?$q$, $q$[{"text":"$\\pi r^2$","is_correct":true},{"text":"$\\pi r$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"dropped-the-apothem"},{"text":"$\\frac{1}{2}\\pi r^2$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"used-half-circumference-as-perimeter"},{"text":"$2\\pi r^2$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"forgot-one-half"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$$\pi r^2$$q$, $q$Substitute the limits into the polygon formula: $\frac{1}{2} \cdot 2\pi r \cdot r = \pi r^2$. This limit argument is why the area of a circle is $\pi r^2$. The value $2\pi r^2$ multiplies perimeter by apothem without the factor $\frac{1}{2}$.$q$, 'draft', null)
