   $q$A circle has radius $1$, so its circumference is $2\pi$. A regular hexagon inscribed in the circle has perimeter $6$, and a square drawn around the circle, touching it on all four sides, has perimeter $8$. Since the circumference lies between these two perimeters, which bounds on $\pi$ follow?$q$, $q$[{"text":"$12 < \\pi < 16$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"multiplied-by-diameter"},{"text":"$6 < \\pi < 8$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"forgot-to-divide-by-diameter"},{"text":"$3 < \\pi < 4$","is_correct":true},{"text":"$1.5 < \\pi < 2$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"divided-by-twice-the-diameter"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$$3 < \pi < 4$$q$, $q$From $6 < 2\pi < 8$, divide every part by $2$, the diameter, to get $3 < \pi < 4$. The bounds $6 < \pi < 8$ compare the perimeters to $\pi$ itself instead of to the circumference $2\pi$.$q$, 'draft', null)
