   $q$A town's population is modeled by $P = 400e^{0.03t}$, where $t$ is years since the town was founded. To the nearest hundredth of a year, when does the population reach 1,000?$q$, $q$[{"text":"$213.23$ years","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"subtracted-initial-amount"},{"text":"$0.03$ years","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"multiplied-by-the-rate"},{"text":"$30.54$ years","is_correct":true},{"text":"$13.26$ years","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"used-common-log-for-base-e"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$$30.54$ years$q$, $q$Set $400e^{0.03t} = 1000$ and divide by $400$: $e^{0.03t} = 2.5$. Take the natural log: $0.03t = \ln 2.5$, so $t = \dfrac{\ln 2.5}{0.03} \approx \dfrac{0.9163}{0.03} \approx 30.54$ years. The answer $13.26$ years uses $\log 2.5$, but base $e$ needs $\ln$.$q$, 'draft', null)
